From df30ee2c3deb7ddc00fc475c7a4882157c06acbe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:39:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(kanban-sync): hub metrics/CPU reports go out on
 an anchored sender thread, independent of round length

Why: after cb1df17c2b the Board stores every hub CPU triple, but a read-only /v2/machines probe saw only
20 / 15 / 20 CPU-bearing reports per 300 s (needs >= 25). The sync loop called Reporter.report_due once per
iteration, after sync_all_once + reconcile_links, so a long round held every report; the loop wait ignored
next_at; and report_due sets next_at = entry + REPORT_EVERY_S, so a late call pushes the next one later and a
call that is earlier relative to its period than the one before is dropped (median gap ~12 s, stalls 18-39 s).

What (card t_01a1cfabc925857b, design S1-S6 plus design-review t_01a1cfaf511c3d9a S-1..S-4):
- hermes_cli/machine_metrics_sender.py (new): MetricsSender, a daemon thread named `metrics-sender` (unique within
  the 15-byte comm limit) that the loop starts beside the AdvisorySampler and closes (bounded 2.5 s join) in the
  same finally. Tick k is due at t0 + k*REPORT_EVERY_S (monotonic). An overrun skips the anchors it covered (no
  burst). Each tick reads the root limit. ON runs the Reporter's unchanged body via Reporter.report_tick; not ON
  does nothing. No exception ends the thread. Runs in a copy of the loop thread's contextvars. Not started on
  non-Linux.
- S-1 due rule: report_tick does not reuse report_due's next_at rule. A tick is due at its anchor; the only guards
  are another report in flight (non-blocking lock), a report start on either path less than REPORT_EVERY_S / 2
  ago (started_at), and the Board's 404/405 backoff. Test: 100 out-of-order wake times -> 100 sends (the old rule
  drops some on the same data).
- S-2 the loop's check (sender.loop_report) is in-memory only: alive, ON at the sender's latest tick, and that tick
  finished within 3 periods. No config read, no Reporter lock (test: 1000 checks with root_limit raising and the
  lock held). While delegated it returns False (component-free not_sent rep, no schema change). Otherwise it is
  exactly report_due: gate off, non-Linux, dead or stalled sender keep loop behaviour. A stalled sender logs one
  WARNING when the loop takes reporting back.
- S-3 every sender send (with or without the CPU triple, 400 compatibility retries included) goes through
  kanban_sync_client._bounded_call: private Hub/connection, CPU_REPORT_BUDGET_S = 2 s wall, never resent. The loop's
  kept-alive hub (HTTP_TIMEOUT_S 30 s) is never used by the sender. A 400 retry is another bounded send, so one
  report is at most 3 x 2 s; ordinary reports make one.
- S-4 one INFO line per attempted send: `metrics_sender send t0= t1= (monotonic ns) end_ms= win_ms= out=
  written= run_ns= wait_ns= worker_run_ns= worker_wait_ns=` (schedstat of the sender thread over the send and of the
  bounded-call worker). Cumulative `metrics_sender ticks=N sent=N failed=N skipped=N` at most every 60 s and at
  close. _bounded_call takes an optional probe dict; DeadlineRequests moved to kanban_sync_bounded_probe.py
  (with watch_written/schedstat) to keep kanban_sync_client.py under its 2000-line cap.

Thread safety of what a sender tick reads (checked): active_card_runs and throughput counters open their own
read-only sqlite connections per call; the throughput cache is a module global rebound atomically to a tuple (a
race only recomputes); drain holds, /proc and disk usage are plain reads; AdvisorySampler.take holds its lock;
kanban_sync_timing spans/note_cpu_sample no-op off the loop thread; Hub keeps one connection per thread and the
sender uses a private Hub per send.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_machine_metrics_sender.py (new, 23: a 30-period blocked round through the real loop
yields >= 3 sends from the sender only; no drift over 100 jittered ticks; out-of-order wakes; overrun skip;
ON->OFF->ON, sender death and stall hand-over spacing + continuity; startup anchor skip; raising collect/send/tick
keep the thread alive; close < 3 s with a send in flight; comm name; log cadence; per-send line incl. 400 retry;
a triple-less send against a hung Board is cut at 2 s; delegated vs full rep records; non-Linux).
14/14 source mutants killed. test_kanban_sync_timing_loop: its gate-ON real-loop instrumentation test disables the
sender start so it keeps exercising the loop's own report path (assertions unchanged).
